<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Intelligent Robots</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Technology Changes Society</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Recognize the writer's point of view in various texts. Reading: Comprehend detailed technical instructions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can recognize a writer's viewpoint on AI in articles, noting positive or cautious perspectives (e.g., "The author is optimistic about AI's contribution to financial automation, such as Panama's advancements in fintech, but warns about job displacement in customer service roles.") Can comprehend technical instructions for AI software, explaining steps to peers (e.g., "The manual instructs users to set up an algorithm and test it with data.") Can summarize technical details for classmates, ensuring understanding of AI concepts (e.g., "The software learns from data inputs to improve its predictions.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f0f0;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images showing various applications of artificial intelligence (AI) and `robots` in daily life (e.g., self-driving cars, automated customer service chatbots, smart home `systems`, factory `robots`). Students are asked to briefly discuss with a partner: "What are the first three words that come to mind when you see these images?" and "Do you think these `technologies` create more `opportunities` or `challenges` for `society`?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to express predictions and viewpoints using conditional forms and future simple. For example, pointing to an image of a `robot` in a `job market` setting, the teacher says: "If `AI continues to evolve`, it `might change the job market significantly` for `customer service` roles. I predict that `AI will create new employment opportunities` in `programming languages` and `software` `development`." The teacher explicitly pronounces words like `robot`, `job`, `start` (`/ɑː/` sound) and `innovative` (`/ˈɪn.ə.veɪ.tɪv/`) and `technological` (`/ˌtek.nəˈlɒdʒ.ɪ.kəl/`), emphasizing vowel reduction in multisyllabic words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we focusing on what has already happened, or what might happen with `technology`?" (Expected answer: What might happen/future.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'point of view,' am I asking for facts or opinions about `AI`?" (Expected answer: Opinions/perspectives.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Intelligent Robots".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short article excerpt titled "The Double-Edged Sword of `Intelligent Robots` in `Society`" (Genre: Online Tech Article; Register: Formal, informative; Format: Printed handout/digital text). The article discusses `AI`'s impact on `job markets` and `customer service`, presenting both optimistic (`opportunities`, `advancements`) and cautious (`challenges`, `displacement`) perspectives. It includes examples of `AI systems` being `programmed` to `learn` from `data` and perform tasks, as well as `conditional forms` and `future simple` statements regarding `technological` `evolution`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Recognize the writer's point of view in various texts. Reading: Comprehend detailed technical instructions (within context).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the article quickly and answer: "What is the primary debate or discussion about `intelligent robots` in this article?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read the article again to determine if a series of statements are True or False (e.g., "The author believes `AI` will eliminate all `employment`." "Some `AI systems` are designed to `enhance` human capabilities rather than replace them.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching Perspectives:</b> Students match phrases or sentences from the article to one of two categories: "Positive Outlook on `AI`" or "Cautious View on `AI`." (e.g., "AI will create new employment opportunities." vs. "If `AI` `advances` rapidly, `societal` `shifts` `might` be `drastic`.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Conditional forms (Type 1 and 2), Future simple, Passive voice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> `artificial intelligence`, `robots`, `technology`, `society`, `opportunities`, `challenges`, `job markets`, `employment`, `algorithms`, `data`, `software`, `functionality`, `systems`, `updates`, `improvements`, `careers`, `customer service`, `experiences`, `programming languages`, `guidelines`, `advance`, `evolve`, `revolutionize`, `adapt`, `create`, `enhance`, `personalize`, `integrate`, `prepare`, `learn`, `apply`, `improve`, `program`, `control`, `update`, `predict`, `enable`, `develop`, `establish`, `shift`, `intelligent`, `artificial`, `responsible`, `complex`, `societal`, `drastic`, `significant`, `evolving`, `future`, `revolutionary`, `personalized`, `controlled`, `passive`, `functional`, `enhanced`, `innovative`, `technological`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice /ɑː/ (robot, job, start), vowel reduction (innovative, technological).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Cloze Reading):</b> Students are given a modified version of the article from Stage 2 with key vocabulary words (e.g., `algorithms`, `functionality`, `evolve`, `predict`) and specific grammar structures (e.g., verbs for `passive voice`, conditional conjunctions) removed. They work individually to fill in the blanks using a provided word bank that includes the target `vocabulary` and appropriate grammatical forms. The teacher monitors and offers assistance, focusing on the correct form and meaning, and models the pronunciation of selected words like "programmed" and "data."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Transformation & Technical Details):</b> In pairs, students receive a short paragraph describing a simple AI `software` `update` or the `functionality` of an `intelligent` `robot`. They must identify sentences that express a point of view or a technical instruction. Then, they rewrite these sentences using different target `vocabulary` or `conditional forms`, while maintaining the original meaning. For example, "The new `software` `improves` `customer service` `experiences`" could become "The new `software` `is developed to enhance` `customer service` `experiences`." or "If `users apply` the `update`, their `experiences` `will improve`." They also practice explaining a technical instruction using the passive voice, e.g., "The `system` `is programmed to learn` from `data` inputs."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Point-of-View Check):</b> Students exchange their rewritten sentences and technical explanations from activity 2. Each partner reads the other's work and asks `pragmatic` questions to check for clarity and accuracy of `point of view` or technical comprehension. For instance, "How `will AI change the job market` according to this sentence?" or "Can you explain the main `guideline` for setting up this `algorithm` to me?" They provide feedback on the use of `vocabulary`, `grammar`, and `clarity`. (Sociolinguistic: Asking about `technology` thoughtfully).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-17 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Intelligent Robots".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "AI in Panama: Technical Brief & Perspective Summary"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives two short texts: (1) an excerpt from a technical manual for an `AI`-powered `software` `system` used in Panama (e.g., for `fintech` or `customer service`), detailing `guidelines` for `setting up` an `algorithm` and `testing` it with `data`; and (2) a short opinion piece from a Panamanian tech blog discussing the `societal` impact of `intelligent robots` on `job markets` in Panama. The opinion piece presents a specific viewpoint (e.g., optimistic about `innovation` or cautious about `employment` `shifts`).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Read & Analyze:</b> Groups read both texts, identifying key technical `instructions` (e.g., "The `AI systems are programmed` to `learn` from `data` to `improve` `predictions`.") and the writer's `point of view` in the opinion piece. They highlight `conditional forms` (e.g., "If `data` `is not clean`, the `algorithm` `might produce` inaccurate results."), `future simple` (e.g., "`AI will revolutionize` `career` `opportunities` in `Panama`."), and `passive voice` (e.g., "`New `systems` `are being developed` to `personalize` `customer service experiences`.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Prepare Summary:</b> Each group prepares a concise, two-part oral summary (2-3 minutes) for their peers:
                        <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part A: Technical Explanation:</b> They explain the `software` `functionality` or the steps to `set up` the `algorithm` to another group. (e.g., "The manual instructs users to set up an `algorithm` by first collecting sufficient `data`, and then `testing` it rigorously.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part B: Point of View Summary:</b> They summarize the writer's `point of view` on `AI`'s `societal` impact in Panama, citing specific textual evidence (e.g., "The author `is optimistic` about `AI's contribution` to `financial automation`, but `warns` about `job displacement`."). They also discuss "How `might AI change jobs in Panama`?" (Sociolinguistic).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Presentation:</b> Groups present their summaries to one another. During the presentations, they are encouraged to `share predictions about technology` (e.g., "AI could improve efficiency in many fields.") and `discuss AI's societal role sensitively`.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during the group summaries in Stage 4. Additionally, groups provide brief written or oral peer feedback to each other after their presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes and articulates the writer's `point of view` (positive/cautious).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehends and explains `detailed technical instructions` accurately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target `vocabulary` (`artificial intelligence`, `algorithms`, `software`, `functionality`, `job markets`, `opportunities`, `challenges`, `society`) appropriately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Applies `conditional forms`, `future simple`, and `passive voice` correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of key words (e.g., `robot`, `job`, `innovative`, `technological`) is intelligible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarizes details clearly and concisely.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates effectively within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new `technical detail` about `AI software` that you understood today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which `writer's viewpoint` on `AI` in `society` did you find most convincing, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which aspects of `reading` for a `writer's point of view` or `technical instructions` were most challenging for you, and how did you try to `adapt`?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has this lesson changed your personal `perspective` on `intelligent robots` and `how technology changes society`?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The `reading` activities successfully engaged students in identifying `viewpoints` and `technical details`. Most students were able to grasp the different perspectives on `AI`'s `societal` impact and understand the general `guidelines` for `software functionality`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some students struggled with `comprehending detailed technical instructions` when the `vocabulary` became very specific to `algorithms` or `data` `processing`. For future lessons, more explicit pre-teaching of highly specialized `technical vocabulary` might be needed, or using simpler diagrams to illustrate processes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The `pronunciation` of multisyllabic words with vowel reduction, like `innovative` and `technological`, will require ongoing practice. Students demonstrated good use of `conditional forms` and `future simple` when making predictions, but `passive voice` requires more reinforcement in active usage.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In our next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), we will `evolve` these discussions by actively `debating` the `opportunities` and `challenges` `intelligent robots` present for `society`, using the `viewpoints` and `technical details` we have `read` and understood today. This will involve using the `vocabulary` and `grammatical structures` in interactive `speaking` tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>